My feet are bare. Someone has strapped my ankles.\") mirrors Grace's fragmented consciousness — short declarative sentences enact cognitive reconstruction\n- The phrase **\"Think.\"** — a single-word imperative paragraph — is Grace commanding himself, establishing that his will is intact even as memory is not\n- The catalogue of sensory details (feet, ceiling, tubes, curved walls) shows a mind working empirically from the bottom up — already foreshadowing his scientist's methodology\n- **\"The silence is worse\"** — the AI voice asking questions was disturbing; its absence is more disturbing still. This introduces existential isolation as a theme immediately.\n\n---\n\n### ⚠️ A Note on the Rocky Passage\n\nThe tool returned the same opening excerpt when asked for the Rocky first-contact passage — it was unable to locate and retrieve that specific later section of the book. For your close-reading analysis of Grace's first encounter with Rocky, **I'd recommend:**\n\n1. **Checking your physical copy** (it occurs roughly in the middle of the novel, around Chapters 10–12, when Grace detects the other ship)\n2. Searching your **library's digital catalog** — many libraries offer *Project Hail Mary* via Libby/OverDrive as an e-book or audiobook, which you can access immediately on your phone\n3. The scene you want begins with Grace detecting an anomalous radar contact and culminates in his first acoustic exchange with Rocky, whose speech Weir renders as musical tones that Grace eventually realizes are a base-5 number system\n\n---\n\n## Summary for Your Paper\n\n*Project Hail Mary* is an exceptionally well-constructed use of amnesia as a **structural and thematic device** — not merely a plot trick. Grace's recovery of memory is the novel's spine: it controls pacing, creates dramatic irony, and forces the reader into radical empathy with a protagonist who must discover his own heroism alongside us. The amnesia also serves the themes of **sacrifice and identity** — who is Ryland Grace when stripped of all context? The answer the novel proposes is: a scientist who solves problems. That, Weir argues, is enough to save two worlds.",
